{
  "dom_id": "step-q280198",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097284/assets/23fa9b0f268e953cf9a2b770c3a3cb83c64fa0217a55e3a5313c81bf6933fc0a.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the above parabola. Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(1,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,2),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =2pt \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{4p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(\\frac{1}{8})t,\\,y=(\\frac{1}{8}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}.\n$$",
